{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nUpper-bound wall-clock assertions in tests are the proven CI flake class under runner contention (19d7, 5e94, edfe, 85c3). 15 sites scanned: 13 upper-bound in scope, 2 lower-bound out of scope; 11 currently unescaped. Detector prototype (regex + escapes) caught all, 0 FPs, and found one site manual scan missed (test_lock_stale_reclaim.py:127).\n\n## Approach\nDET BLOCKING code-review criterion scanning tests/** for upper-bound wall-clock asserts. Escapes: '# timing: hang-guard — <reason>' line marker, or the existing perf-lane CI-exclusion guard '@pytest.mark.skipif(os.environ.get(\"CI\") == \"true\", ...)' on the test (the idiom the benchmark tests in tests/scripts/reducer/test_reducer_cache.py:245-249 already use — NOTE: the '@pytest.mark.benchmark' marker alone is NOT an escape — it is registered (pyproject.toml, informational) but no CI invocation filters it out (the suite runs -m 'not integration and not external'), so a benchmark-marked test still executes on CI; the skipif(CI) guard is what actually removes runner-contention flake risk). Migrations (proven by experiment): tests/unit/test_config_perf.py:201 -> spy config._resolve miss path (reset_config_cache seam; regression sim caught 10/10); tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py:261 -> exactly one pending_enrichment call + zero drain/spawn. Remaining 9 sites get markers with reasons. Optional follow-on: migrate reducer benchmarks to _api.replay_events spy to restore CI coverage (currently benchmark-skipped). Seed implementation from /tmp/exp4_detector.py; proxy rule: spy the MISS-PATH function, never tomllib (warm TOML cache evades parse spies).\n\n## Scope\n- detector as DET code-review criterion (review-bot checkout) or CI lint per gate conventions\n- tests/unit/test_config_perf.py:201, tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py:261 migrations\n- markers on the 9 hang-guard sites\n\n## Testing\nDetector unit tests: unescaped upper-bound assert -> finding; hang-guard marker escape -> silent; skipif(CI) guard escape -> silent (bare '@pytest.mark.benchmark' without the skipif guard -> still a finding); lower-bound assert -> silent. Migrated tests: regression simulation goes RED (cache cleared per call / drain forced), warm path GREEN.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Detector blocks new unescaped upper-bound wall-clock asserts in tests/** with a teaching message naming both escapes (hang-guard marker; skipif-CI perf-lane guard).\n- [ ] Both migration sites assert counting proxies (miss-path spies), proven RED under regression simulation.\n- [ ] All remaining in-scope sites carry reasoned markers; detector run on the tree is clean.\n- [ ] Detector has unit tests covering fire, both escapes (marker; skipif-CI guard — and bare benchmark marker NOT escaping), and lower-bound exclusion."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785803034442527001,"uuid":"f8407651-73a6-4aac-8a60-052d4377aff4"}